ong> The convolutional stages hold only about 15M. The three fully connected layers (25088→4096→4096→1000) hold roughly 123M — nearly 90% of the model sits in the classifier head. This is why modern networks replaced the FC stack with global average pooling (Chapter 3): VGG's convolutions were the valuable part; its classifier was parameter bloat. When you use VGG as a feature extractor today, you keep the 15M parameters that matter and discard the 123M that don't — a vivid illustration of transfer learning's selectivity, and a good answer to "why not just use the whole pretrained model."</p>
<p><strong>Loading the classics today.</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-python">from torchvision import models
vgg = models.vgg16(weights=models.VGG16_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)
alex = models.alexnet(weights=models.AlexNet_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)
print(vgg.features[0])  # Conv2d(3, 64, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding=(1, 1))
# Feature extraction: drop the classifier, keep the conv stages
features = vgg.features  # -&gt; (B, 512, 7, 7) for 224px input
</code></pre>
<p>Note the weights enums carry version information (IMAGENET1K_V1 vs V2) — V2 weights are retrained with a modern recipe and score several points higher on the same architecture. "VGG-16, torchvision IMAGENET1K_V1" is a complete, reproducible specification; "we used VGG" is not. Record the version.</p>
<p><strong>What the classics get wrong by modern standards.</strong> No batch normalization (training is twitchier and slower), heavy FC heads (parameter bloat, as shown above), and large first-layer kernels (AlexNet's 11×11 stride-4 stem discards fine detail before the network ever sees it — VGG's stacked 3×3s were already the fix). Study these networks for their patterns, not their hyperparameters. And a terminology note for your writing: "AlexNet" denotes the specific 2012 architecture; "AlexNet-style" in a paper usually just means "a shallow convnet with ReLUs and dropout" — be precise about which you mean, because a reviewer will hold you to the specific one.</p>
<h3 id="what-survived-the-pattern-inventory-from-lenet-to-vgg">What survived: the pattern inventory from LeNet to VGG</h3>
<p>It's worth making explicit which ideas from these three networks are still alive in 2026, because "we use a VGG-style backbone" is a claim about <em>which</em> patterns you kept:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Pattern</th>
<th>Source</th>
<th>Still standard?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Conv→pool alternation building a hierarchy</td>
<td>LeNet</td>
<td>Yes — every CNN</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>End-to-end learned features beating hand-designed ones</td>
<td>LeNet</td>
<td>Yes — the field's founding result</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ReLU as default nonlinearity</td>
<td>AlexNet</td>
<td>Yes (variants: GELU, SiLU in newer nets)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dropout in classifier heads</td>
<td>AlexNet</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data augmentation as standard practice</td>
<td>AlexNet</td>
<td>Yes — non-negotiable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>GPU training</td>
<td>AlexNet</td>
<td>Yes — now table stakes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stacked 3×3 instead of large kernels</td>
<td>VGG</td>
<td>Yes — the default kernel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Channel doubling when halving spatial size (64→128→256→512)</td>
<td>VGG</td>
<td>Yes — near-universal</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Giant FC classifier heads</td>
<td>VGG/AlexNet</td>
<td>No — replaced by GAP</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>LRN, split-GPU training</td>
<td>AlexNet</td>
<td>No — historical curiosities</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Two networks <em>between</em> the classics deserve one paragraph each, because reviewers may ask why you skipped them. <strong>Network-in-Network</strong> (Lin et al., 2014) introduced the 1×1 convolution and global average pooling — both now ubiquitous (Chapters 2–3). <strong>GoogLeNet/Inception</strong> (Szegedy et al., 2015) ran multiple kernel sizes (1×1, 3×3, 5×5) in parallel inside each module, arguing that optimal local structure varies by scale; it won ImageNet 2014 with far fewer parameters than VGG. Its legacy: multi-scale processing (see ASPP, Chapter 3) and the 1×1 bottleneck-before-expensive-conv pattern. You don't need to use Inception today, but "multi-scale features" as a phrase descends from it.</p>
<p><strong>Reading old papers productively.</strong> When you read Krizhevsky et al. (2012), skip the LRN sections and focus on the training recipe (augmentation, dropout, SGD schedule) — the recipe outlived the architecture. When you read Simonyan &amp; Zisserman (2014), focus on the 3×3-stacking argument and the depth study (their Table 3, showing accuracy vs. depth, is the direct ancestor of every ablation table you'll write). Old papers are mines: extract the patterns, leave the hyperparameters.</p>
