<p>Read down the "what it established" column: that is the entire design vocabulary of modern CNNs. Every architecture since 2019 is a recombination of these rows. When a new paper claims novelty, locate its row-combination — "it's ResNet blocks + SE + compound scaling" — and you'll understand the contribution in one sentence. This is also how you write related work (Chapter 12): position by idea-lineage, not by year.</p>
<p><strong>Key takeaways</strong>
- LeNet: conv-pool alternation + end-to-end learning. The template.
- AlexNet: ReLU, GPUs, dropout, augmentation. The big bang — scale + recipe beat hand-designed features.
- VGG: stacked 3×3 kernels (same RF as 5×5/7×7, fewer params, more nonlinearities); modular blocks; channel doubling after pooling.
- Each architecture is a bundle of reusable patterns. Learn the patterns, not the parameter counts.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="chapter-5-resnet-and-skip-connections">Chapter 5 — ResNet and Skip Connections</h2>
<h3 id="the-degradation-problem">The degradation problem</h3>
<p>By 2015, the field knew depth helped — but only to a point. Researchers observed something strange: a 56-layer plain network had <strong>higher training error</strong> than a 20-layer one. Not test error — <em>training</em> error. This wasn't overfitting (overfitting is low training error, high test error). The deeper network was failing to even fit the training data. Adding layers made optimization harder, not just generalization.</p>
<p>He et al.'s key insight: suppose a shallow network reaches some good solution. A deeper network <em>contains</em> the shallow one as a special case — it could just learn identity mappings in the extra layers and match the shallow performance. The fact that it can't, in practice, means the optimizer struggles to learn identity mappings through stacked nonlinear layers. So: <strong>make identity the default, and learn only the deviation from it</strong>.</p>
<h3 id="the-residual-trick">The residual trick</h3>
<p>Instead of asking a stack of layers to learn the desired mapping H(x) directly, ask it to learn the <strong>residual</strong> F(x) = H(x) − x, and add the input back:</p>
<p><strong>y = F(x) + x</strong></p>
<p>That "+" is the <strong>skip connection</strong> (or shortcut): the input bypasses the layer stack and is added to its output. If the optimal mapping is close to identity, the layers just drive F(x) toward zero — pushing weights to zero is easy for an optimizer (weight decay does it by default). If the optimal mapping is far from identity, the network still learns the full transformation through F.</p>
<p>Why does this fix the vanishing gradient? Look at the backward pass. The gradient of the loss with respect to x flows through two paths: through F (the long way, through all the layer weights) and directly through the skip connection (gradient = 1, untouched). Even if the long path's gradients vanish to nothing, the skip path delivers a clean gradient of 1 to every earlier layer. <strong>Skip connections are gradient highways.</strong> Information — and, crucially, gradient signal — can travel the full depth of the network without passing through a single weight matrix if needed.</p>
<p>A residual block in its simplest form:</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">class BasicBlock(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, channels):
        super().__init__()
        self.conv1 = nn.Conv2d(channels, channels, 3, padding=1, bias=False)
        self.bn1 = nn.BatchNorm2d(channels)
        self.conv2 = nn.Conv2d(channels, channels, 3, padding=1, bias=False)
        self.bn2 = nn.BatchNorm2d(channels)
        self.relu = nn.ReLU(inplace=True)

    def forward(self, x):
        identity = x
        out = self.relu(self.bn1(self.conv1(x)))
        out = self.bn2(self.conv2(out))
        out = out + identity   # the skip connection
        return self.relu(out)
</code></pre>
<p>ResNet-152 (152 layers!) trained successfully with this pattern and won ImageNet 2015 at 3.57% top-5 error — below the commonly cited ~5% human error rate. The degradation problem was gone: ResNet-152 had <em>lower</em> training error than ResNet-34.</p>
<h3 id="when-dimensions-dont-match">When dimensions don't match</h3>
<p>The skip connection needs x and F(x) to have the same shape. When a block changes spatial size (stride 2) or channel count, ResNet uses a <strong>projection shortcut</strong>: a 1×1 convolution with matching stride on the skip path:</p>
<pre><code class="language-python"># downsampling block: stride-2 conv + 1x1 projection on the shortcut
self.shortcut = nn.Sequential(
    nn.Conv2d(in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size=1, stride=2, bias=False),
    nn.BatchNorm2d(out_ch))
</code></pre>
<p>The 1×1 convolution here does no spatial work — it just remaps channels and matches the stride so the addition lines up. (This is the same "1×1 as channel mixer" idea from Chapter 2.)</p>
<h3 id="batch-normalization-the-quiet-partner">Batch normalization: the quiet partner</h3>
<p>Every ResNet block pairs convolutions with <strong>batch normalization</strong>: normalize each channel to zero mean, unit variance across the batch, then scale and shift with learned parameters. BN stabilizes the distribution of layer inputs during training (reducing "internal covariate shift," in the original paper's framing), which lets you use higher learning rates and makes deep stacks trainable at all. Practical notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>BN behaves differently in train vs. eval mode (it uses batch statistics when training, running averages at inference). <strong>Always call <code>model.eval()</code> before inference</strong>, or your predictions will be wrong in a subtle, batch-dependent way. This is the single most common ResNet bug in student code.</li>
<li>With very small batch sizes (&lt; 8–16), batch statistics are noisy and BN hurts; use GroupNorm or LayerNorm instead — common in medical imaging where GPU memory forces tiny batches.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="resnet-variants-youll-meet">ResNet variants you'll meet</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>ResNet-18/34</strong> use BasicBlocks (two 3×3 convs). <strong>ResNet-50/101/152</strong> use bottleneck blocks (1×1 → 3×3 → 1×1), which cut computation: the 1×1s shrink then restore channel counts around the expensive 3×3.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-activation ResNet</strong> (He et al., 2016): move BN+ReLU before the convolution. Slightly better gradient flow; some codebases use it by default.</li>
<li><strong>ResNeXt</strong>: grouped convolutions in the block ("cardinality" as a new dimension). <strong>Wide ResNet</strong>: fewer, wider layers — often better than deeper-thinner for the same budget.</li>
</ul>
<p>For new work, ResNet-50 is the default backbone: 25.6M parameters, ~4 GFLOPs, pretrained weights available everywhere, and every reviewer knows what it means.</p>
<h3 id="worked-example-gradient-through-the-skip">Worked example: gradient through the skip</h3>
<p>Consider y = F(x) + x with a scalar loss L. By the chain rule:</p>
<p>dL/dx = dL/dy × (dF/dx + 1)</p>
<p>Even if dF/dx → 0 (vanished through the block's weights), dL/dx = dL/dy × 1 = dL/dy. The gradient passes through unchanged. Stack 50 such blocks: the gradient to the first layer is dL/dy_50 × Π(1 + dF_i/dx_i) — a product of terms each near 1, instead of a product of small numbers. That's the whole mathematical story of why 152 layers train.</p>
<p><img alt="CNN architecture stack" src="media-generation-book-12-fig2-0-546ebea2-d712-47ea-8a3f-c45419143d98.webp" /></p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>For your research.</strong> ResNet-50 pretrained on ImageNet is your default starting point for almost any image task (Chapter 7 explains how to adapt it). When you report results, "ResNet-50 backbone" is unambiguous — but specify the weight source (torchvision, timm) because pretrained weights differ slightly across implementations and affect reproducibility. If your images are very different from natural photos (grayscale X-rays, multispectral satellite), say so explicitly and consider whether the early-layer filters transfer (they usually do — edges are edges) while the later layers need more adaptation. The skip-connection gradient argument above is worth understanding deeply: it's the same mechanism behind DenseNet, U-Net's long skips, and transformer residual streams. Learn it once, reuse it everywhere.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="the-bottleneck-math-and-the-resnet-family-tree">The bottleneck math, and the ResNet family tree</h3>
<p><strong>Worked: why bottlenecks save compute.</strong> A BasicBlock operating on 256 channels uses two 3×3×256×256 convolutions: 2 × 9 × 256 × 256 ≈ 1.18M parameters. A bottleneck block instead does: 1×1 (256→64) + 3×3 (64→64) + 1×1 (64→256) = 16,384 + 36,864 + 16,384 ≈ 69K parameters — <strong>17× fewer</strong> for the same input/output width, with the expensive 3×3 operating in the cheap 64-channel space. ResNet-50's 25.6M parameters would exceed 200M with basic blocks at that depth; bottlenecks are what make 50+ layers affordable. The general principle: <strong>do expensive spatial work in low-dimensional spaces, and use cheap 1×1 convolutions to move between widths.</strong> You'll meet this pattern again in MobileNetV2 (Chapter 6) and in transformer feedforward layers — it's universal enough to be worth memorizing.</p>
<p><strong>The family tree — patterns to steal.</strong>
- <strong>Pre-activation ResNet</strong> (He et al., 2016): reorder each block to BN→ReLU→Conv, so the skip path is a <em>pure</em> identity unobstructed by ReLU. Slightly better optimization; some codebases (e.g., <code>timm</code>'s ResNet-V2 variants) default to it.
- <strong>Wide ResNet</strong> (Zagoruyko &amp; Komodakis, 2016): widen channels (×2 to ×10) instead of deepening. A 28-layer wide ResNet often beats a 100-layer thin one at the same parameter budget — depth has diminishing returns once skip connections fix the optimization problem. Lesson: when a deeper model stalls, try wider before trying deeper.
- <strong>ResNeXt</strong> (Xie et al., 2017): split the 3×3 convolution into 32 parallel groups ("cardinality" as a new scaling dimension). Same FLOPs, more representational paths. The grouped convolution here descends from AlexNet's split-GPU accident (Chapter 4) — an engineering constraint become a design principle, again.
- <strong>Stochastic Depth</strong> (Huang et al., 2016): during training, randomly drop entire residual blocks — the skip connection carries the signal past them. This regularizes very deep networks and shortens the effective depth early in training. One line in <code>timm</code>; worth trying on any 100+ layer model.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">class Bottleneck(nn.Module):
    expansion = 4
    def __init__(self, in_ch, mid_ch, stride=1):
        super().__init__()
        self.conv1 = nn.Conv2d(in_ch, mid_ch, 1, bias=False)
        self.conv2 = nn.Conv2d(mid_ch, mid_ch, 3, stride=stride, padding=1, bias=False)
        self.conv3 = nn.Conv2d(mid_ch, mid_ch * self.expansion, 1, bias=False)
        self.bn1 = nn.BatchNorm2d(mid_ch)
        self.bn2 = nn.BatchNorm2d(mid_ch)
        self.bn3 = nn.BatchNorm2d(mid_ch * self.expansion)
        self.relu = nn.ReLU(inplace=True)
        self.shortcut = nn.Sequential()
        if stride != 1 or in_ch != mid_ch * self.expansion:
            self.shortcut = nn.Sequential(
                nn.Conv2d(in_ch, mid_ch * self.expansion, 1, stride=stride, bias=False),
                nn.BatchNorm2d(mid_ch * self.expansion))
    def forward(self, x):
        out = self.relu(self.bn1(self.conv1(x)))
        out = self.relu(self.bn2(self.conv2(out)))
        out = self.bn3(self.conv3(out))
        return self.relu(out + self.shortcut(x))
</code></pre>
<p><strong>ResNet beyond classification.</strong> The same backbone-with-skips serves as the encoder in U-Net-style segmentation (Chapter 11), the feature extractor in Faster R-CNN detection, and the image tower in CLIP. Learning ResNet deeply pays rent across every vision task — which is why this book keeps recommending it as your default backbone. Time spent understanding skip connections is never wasted: the identical mechanism reappears in DenseNet's dense skips, U-Net's long skips, and transformer residual streams.</p>
<h3 id="densenet-and-the-vanishing-gradient-in-equations">DenseNet, and the vanishing gradient in equations</h3>
<p><strong>DenseNet: skips taken to the extreme.</strong> If ResNet adds the input to the output (y = F(x) + x), DenseNet (Huang et al., 2017) <em>concatenates</em> all previous feature maps as input to each layer: layer l receives [x_0, x_1, ..., x_{l−1}]. Every layer talks directly to every later layer — maximum gradient flow, maximum feature reuse. Parameter-efficient (narrow layers suffice since features are reused, not relearned), but memory-hungry (all those concatenated maps live on GPU). DenseNet-121 remains a strong, well-understood backbone, especially loved in medical imaging. The design lesson: ResNet reuses via addition (cheap, fixed width), DenseNet via concatenation (richer, memory-costly). When choosing, ask whether your GPU budget favors parameters (DenseNet) or activations memory (ResNet).</p>
<p><strong>The vanishing gradient, in one equation.</strong> In a plain deep stack, the gradient to an early layer is a product of many Jacobians: ∂L/∂x_1 = ∂L/∂x_n × Π_{i} ∂x_{i+1}/∂x_i. If each Jacobian's singular values are mostly &lt; 1 (typical with saturating nonlinearities and small weights), the product shrinks exponentially with depth — 0.9^50 ≈ 0.005. The signal dies. BatchNorm and ReLU mitigate this (ReLU's derivative is 0 or 1, never a shrinking fraction), but the structural fix is the skip: with y = F(x) + x, each factor becomes (∂F/∂x + I), whose eigenvalues cluster near 1 rather than near 0. Products of numbers near 1 don't vanish. That single "+ I" is worth more than any initialization trick — which is why ResNet enabled 152 layers while careful initialization alone stalled around 20–30.</p>
<p><strong>How deep is too deep?</strong> Empirically: ResNet-152 beats ResNet-101 by ~0.5% on ImageNet at 2× the cost; ResNet-1001 (yes, it was tried) barely beats 152. Depth's returns diminish because representational capacity stops being the bottleneck — data and optimization are. For your thesis: ResNet-50 is the sweet spot for most tasks; reach for 101/152 only when you have the data (100K+ images) and the ablations show depth actually helping. "We used ResNet-152 because deeper is better" without evidence is a sentence reviewers underline.</p>
<h3 id="the-original-resnet-training-recipe-and-what-to-keep">The original ResNet training recipe (and what to keep)</h3>
<p>He et al. trained with <strong>SGD, momentum 0.9, weight decay 1e-4, batch size 256, initial LR 0.1, divided by 10 at epochs 30/60</strong> (for the 90-epoch schedule), with scale/aspect-ratio augmentation and per-pixel mean subtraction. No Adam, no cosine, no warmup, no label smoothing — and it hit 3.57% top-5. Two lessons. First, the recipe's <em>structure</em> (long training, step decay, strong augmentation, weight decay) matters more than the specific optimizer — modern recipes are refinements, not replacements. Second, note what they <em>didn't</em> need: the architecture did the heavy lifting. When your training is unstable, the debugging order is: architecture sanity (shapes, RF) → data pipeline → LR/schedule → optimizer choice — roughly in that order of likelihood.</p>
<p><strong>BatchNorm placement, settled.</strong> ResNet V1: Conv→BN→ReLU, with the skip added before the final ReLU. Pre-activation V2: BN→ReLU→Conv with a clean identity skip. If you implement ResNet yourself, use V2 — the pure skip path gives marginally better gradient flow and it's what <code>timm</code>'s resnetv2 variants use. If you use torchvision's, you get V1 — fine, just know which you have when comparing numbers across libraries. "ResNet-50 (torchvision V1 weights)" vs "ResNet-50-V2 (timm)" can differ by ~1%, and that difference is not yours to claim.</p>
<p><strong>Key takeaways</strong>
- Deep plain networks suffer <em>degradation</em>: higher training error than shallow ones — an optimization failure, not overfitting.
- Residual learning: fit F(x) = H(x) − x, output y = F(x) + x. Identity becomes the easy default.
- Skip connections are gradient highways: dL/dx keeps a clean path of gradient 1 through every block.
- BatchNorm is ResNet's quiet partner; remember <code>model.eval()</code> and the small-batch caveat.
- ResNet-50 is the default research backbone. Bottleneck blocks (1×1–3×3–1×1) make depth affordable.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="chapter-6-efficient-modern-cnns-mobilenet-and-efficientnet">Chapter 6 — Efficient Modern CNNs: MobileNet and EfficientNet</h2>
<p>ResNet-50 is a fine backbone — until you need to run on a phone, a drone, a field-deployed Raspberry Pi, or a hospital workstation without a GPU. Or until your thesis budget gives you one GPU and your architecture search needs to finish before graduation. This chapter is about getting the most accuracy per unit of compute.</p>
<h3 id="the-cost-anatomy-of-a-convolution">The cost anatomy of a convolution</h3>
<p>Recall the MAC count: H × W × C_out × K² × C_in. The K² term is the lever. A 3×3 kernel costs 9× a 1×1. And a standard convolution mixes <em>spatial</em> filtering and <em>channel</em> mixing in one dense operation. MobileNet's insight: <strong>factorize them</strong>.</p>
<h3 id="mobilenet-depthwise-separable-convolutions">MobileNet: depthwise separable convolutions</h3>
<p>A <strong>depthwise separable convolution</strong> splits the standard convolution into two steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Depthwise convolution</strong>: each input channel gets its own single K×K filter — no cross-channel mixing. Cost: H × W × C_in × K².</li>
<li><strong>Pointwise convolution</strong>: a 1×1 convolution mixing channels. Cost: H × W × C_in × C_out.</li>
</ol>
<p>Total: HWC_in(K² + C_out) versus HWC_in·K²·C_out for the standard version — a reduction factor of roughly <strong>1/C_out + 1/K²</strong>. For K=3 and C_out=512, that's about <strong>8–9× fewer MACs</strong>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">class DepthwiseSeparableConv(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, in_ch, out_ch, stride=1):
        super().__init__()
        self.depthwise = nn.Conv2d(in_ch, in_ch, kernel_size=3,
                                   stride=stride, padding=1,
                                   groups=in_ch, bias=False)  # groups=in_ch: one filter per channel
        self.pointwise = nn.Conv2d(in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size=1, bias=False)
        self.bn1 = nn.BatchNorm2d(in_ch)
        self.bn2 = nn.BatchNorm2d(out_ch)
        self.relu = nn.ReLU(inplace=True)

    def forward(self, x):
        x = self.relu(self.bn1(self.depthwise(x)))
        return self.relu(self.bn2(self.pointwise(x)))

# Compare costs for 56x56x64 -&gt; 128 channels:
std = nn.Conv2d(64, 128, 3, padding=1)
sep = DepthwiseSeparableConv(64, 128)
print(sum(p.numel() for p in std.parameters()))  # 73,856
print(sum(p.numel() for p in sep.parameters()))  # 8,896  (~8.3x fewer)
</code></pre>
<p>MobileNetV1 (2017) used this throughout and reached ~70% ImageNet top-1 with 4.2M parameters — near-VGG accuracy at a fraction of the cost. <strong>MobileNetV2</strong> (2018) added the <strong>inverted residual block</strong>: expand channels with 1×1, depthwise-filter in the wide space, project back with 1×1 (linear, no ReLU — because ReLU destroys information in low-dimensional bottlenecks, a subtle and important detail). <strong>MobileNetV3</strong> added squeeze-and-excitation and neural-architecture-search-derived tweaks.</p>
<h3 id="efficientnet-scaling-with-a-formula">EfficientNet: scaling with a formula</h3>
<p>Practitioners knew three ways to make a network better: make it <strong>deeper</strong> (more layers), <strong>wider</strong> (more channels), or feed <strong>higher-resolution</strong> inputs. Everyone scaled one dimension by gut feeling. Tan and Le's EfficientNet (2019) showed the dimensions interact and should be scaled <strong>together</strong>, with a compound coefficient φ:</p>
<ul>
<li>depth × α^φ, width × β^φ, resolution × γ^φ, with α·β²·γ² ≈ 2 (so each φ step ≈ 2× FLOPs).</li>
</ul>
<p>They found α=1.2, β=1.1, γ=1.15 by small grid search on a baseline (EfficientNet-B0, itself found by neural architecture search with mobile inverted bottleneck blocks), then scaled φ from 0 (B0) to 7 (B7). EfficientNet-B7 reached 84.3% top-1 — state of the art at the time — with 66M parameters, versus prior SOTA needing far more. More importantly for researchers: <strong>EfficientNet-B0 through B4 give you a clean accuracy-vs-compute ladder</strong>. Need a stronger baseline? Move one rung up the ladder instead of hand-tuning.</p>
<h3 id="the-efficiency-landscape-what-to-pick-when">The efficiency landscape (what to pick when)</h3>
<table>
